#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca0e85f4-3952-5d15-9f69-40aee1a654d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318678} {:answer/id #uuid "80b26806-e29d-5cc5-8c95-1dff48c8d440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318679} {:answer/id #uuid "002ba530-970d-556c-ac60-faf7bb306164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318680} {:answer/id #uuid "76da4510-2ae2-53f3-a554-716fad759912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318681} {:answer/id #uuid "0947e59d-64b1-5170-b90d-ddf468082f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0947e59d-64b1-5170-b90d-ddf468082f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318682} :answer-field/id #uuid "9551c38c-7aa9-56fe-b301-d8ad737a2d2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d48d67e8-58fe-5452-842b-eea462921666" :question/math-academy-id "q-110279" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5)=\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{′}(5){f}^{-1}(5).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=-1,$ $g(5)=-4,$ ${g}^{′}(5)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5) & =\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{′}(5){f}^{-1}(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-5}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7519bb-b47a-57e2-8892-57c435b02cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318685} {:answer/id #uuid "60b0725e-ac88-5a08-b1fb-e1ff58195c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318686} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c72983-c113-5f13-831d-1a3ffaf96573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318687} {:answer/id #uuid "fc957a5f-dd13-5d05-bf73-c78dd249d89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318688} {:answer/id #uuid "51a5a33d-cc2e-523f-8250-36749b6d223d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b0725e-ac88-5a08-b1fb-e1ff58195c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318686} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79fa784-2df7-5911-89a7-a24e4f8099fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77be3a89-d855-50f1-9ab2-8a545dc34af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14770" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10$ and the point $P(0,10)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(10,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,10).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,10)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(10,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(10) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(10))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "900a4ff5-ab72-5fc3-8f2b-ff2a29fe4a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "900a4ff5-ab72-5fc3-8f2b-ff2a29fe4a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318692} :answer-field/id #uuid "978ff4cd-46ce-58c1-b0ec-dd9028e5f23b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b1d6874-7c89-57b5-b0dd-bd93c14e2415" :question/math-academy-id "q-342584" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b535b7-c7b1-5655-9154-19d1882e8024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318695} {:answer/id #uuid "84f02f1c-f89c-5ab0-9cce-bf5fe822eb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318696} {:answer/id #uuid "8e38246e-0702-5e0b-b4bb-3eaf7caae475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318697} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad36b50-c9fb-5e9f-adf8-f9b7d8de536d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318698} {:answer/id #uuid "3593e90c-d3ae-58dc-b59c-336226d5590b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e38246e-0702-5e0b-b4bb-3eaf7caae475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318697} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd2f178-5b3a-5305-bf61-f93e8920577f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d191fb44-ba38-563d-8487-657d2de79864" :question/math-academy-id "q-76112" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=-2$ since the logarithm is not defined for negative numbers. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{2(x+2)}{2x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)+\\frac{2(0+2)}{2\\cdot 0+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =0+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964b338c-ec7b-55f0-8ec9-26e61ddc1a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063700} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa2b59f-1dd2-5c21-9a9f-2e6766820f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063701} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4f157-fee3-5ef6-8e0a-7b24a9c266c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063702} {:answer/id #uuid "7185db66-1354-58fd-ba7e-b77360446aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063703} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4e47e2-72d9-53e1-a30a-690757f708b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7185db66-1354-58fd-ba7e-b77360446aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063703} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2bc766-7d15-5dff-9fa2-341eea84dc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a674864-526f-5399-9fef-530d9892c47d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
